{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Research Translation for Learning Design","title":"Turning Designs Into Study Questions","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting turning designs into study questions to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-01","statement":"Distinguish a design preference (untestable) from a design hypothesis (testable) using the manipulation/measure/comparison test"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-02","statement":"Rewrite a vague storyboard note as a study question with a named independent variable, an observable dependent variable, and a baseline"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-03","statement":"Choose a single primary measure and justify excluding plausible-but-irrelevant outcomes"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-04","statement":"Predict in advance which result would send you back to revise the frame"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_turning_designs_into_study_questions","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":1,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"01","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/06/08"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_annotation_signal"],"ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_calibration"],"ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_calibration"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Turning Designs Into Study Questions\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Turning Designs Into Study Questions, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/07-01-turning-designs-into-study-questions.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Turning Designs Into Study Questions: Turning Designs Into Study Questions, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nMost design decisions arrive disguised as preferences. A storyboard frame \"feels clearer\" with a signaling arrow; narration \"seems better\" before the visual; a control \"should\" sit on the right. None of those statements can be confirmed or disconfirmed, which means none of them can guide a revision you could defend to a committee, a client, or a journal reviewer. Research translation begins the moment you convert a design preference into a study question: a statement narrow enough that a small amount of evidence could change your mind.\n\nThis section teaches the conversion itself. You will take a feature of a storyboard frame sequence (narration timing, a signaling cue, a reader-action prompt) and rewrite the design hope as a falsifiable question that names a manipulation, a measurable learner behavior, and a comparison. The skill matters because a learning designer who cannot state what would prove them wrong is not designing with evidence; they are decorating.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Distinguish a design preference (untestable) from a design hypothesis (testable) using the manipulation/measure/comparison test.\n- Rewrite a vague storyboard note as a study question with a named independent variable, an observable dependent variable, and a baseline.\n- Choose a single primary measure and justify excluding plausible-but-irrelevant outcomes.\n- Predict in advance which result would send you back to revise the frame.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA designer hands you frame 3 of a statistics lesson. The narration explains a residual plot; a new red arrow now points at the simulation's \"Run\" control. The note reads: \"Added an arrow so the lesson is clearer.\" The screen is attractive and the arrow is well placed. Yet the note hides three unanswered questions: clearer for whom, clearer in what observable way, and clearer compared with what? Until those are answered, \"clearer\" is a feeling the designer has, not a property the frame possesses. Your job is to turn that feeling into something a five-learner tryout could actually test.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA study question is the operationalized form of a design claim. It has four moving parts, and a useful question makes all four explicit:\n\n- **Independent variable (the manipulation):** the single design element you change, stated precisely enough to build two versions. \"An arrow\" is vague; \"a red signaling arrow on frame 3, appearing after the narration ends\" is buildable.\n- **Dependent variable (the observable):** the learner behavior you can see and record. Replace internal states (\"understands,\" \"feels confident\") with actions (\"selects the correct label,\" \"time to first click on Run\").\n- **Comparison (the baseline):** what the manipulation is measured against, usually a no-treatment or alternative-treatment version. Without a comparison, any result is uninterpretable.\n- **Population and condition:** who is tested and under what constraint, because the same arrow may help novices and bore experts.\n\nThe course anchors do specific work here. **Mayer's signaling principle** predicts that cueing essential material reduces extraneous search, which tells you the arrow's plausible mechanism and therefore the right measure (search time, not enjoyment). **Cognitive load theory** warns that an added cue is not free: if it competes with the narration for attention, it can raise extraneous load rather than lower it, so your question should be able to detect harm, not only benefit. **UDL learner variability** reminds you that a single primary measure can mask divergent effects, so you specify the population whose behavior the question is really about.\n\nA practical heuristic: if you cannot describe the two screens a developer would build to test your question, the question is still a preference.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Independent variable\",\"definition\":\"The single design element you change, stated precisely enough to build two versions (a red arrow on frame 3, appearing after narration).\"},{\"term\":\"Dependent variable\",\"definition\":\"The observable learner behavior you can see and record (time to first click on Run), replacing internal states.\"},{\"term\":\"Comparison\",\"definition\":\"The baseline the manipulation is measured against, usually an identical no-treatment version, without which any result is uninterpretable.\"},{\"term\":\"Population and condition\",\"definition\":\"Who is tested and under what constraint, because the same arrow may help novices and bore experts.\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A storyboard note reads: 'Make the arrow bigger and a brighter red so it really stands out.' Why is this still a preference, not a study question?\" explanation=\"Adding adjectives sharpens the description of the artifact but supplies no observable measure and no comparison, so no tryout result could confirm or refute it.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It adds detail about the artifact but names no measurable behavior and no comparison.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is testable because a brighter arrow is easier to build.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It names a population, so it is a complete study question.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nFollow one storyboard note as it becomes a question a tryout can answer.\n\n**Before (design note on the storyboard):**\n\n> Frame 3 adds a signaling arrow to point learners toward the simulation control. I think this will make the lesson clearer.\n\n1. \"Clearer\" names no behavior, so no observation could confirm or refute it.\n2. There is no comparison, so even a positive reaction would be uninterpretable.\n3. The mechanism is unnamed, so we cannot pick the right measure.\n\n**After (study question tied to the same frame):**\n\n> Among first-time learners, does placing a red signaling arrow on frame 3 (appearing after the narration ends) reduce the median time to start the first simulation action, compared with an identical frame without the arrow?\n\nThis version names the manipulation (red arrow, frame 3, post-narration), the observable (median time-to-first-action), the comparison (identical no-arrow frame), and the population (first-time learners). A developer could build both screens; a reviewer can see exactly what result would matter. The prespecified decision rule is: if the arrow version does not reduce median time-to-first-action by at least a noticeable margin, the arrow is not earning its place and frame 3 reverts.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Diagnose a half-converted note slot by slot\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"A note reads: \\\"Add a glossary popup so learners on frame 5 understand the term faster, compared with the current frame.\\\" Which of the four slots is still missing?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Manipulation\",\"Observable\",\"Comparison\",\"Population\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The observable is missing. \\\"Understand the term faster\\\" names no recordable behavior. A buildable measure would be time-to-correct on the next item that uses the term, or whether the learner selects the correct definition.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Once you supply that observable, which slot is STILL unfilled in the note as written?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Manipulation\",\"Observable\",\"Population\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"Population. The note never says who is tested. A glossary popup may help first-time learners but bore returning ones, so the question must name the population whose behavior it is really about, for example learners encountering the term for the first time.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Fully converted: \\\"Among learners meeting the term for the first time on frame 5, does a glossary popup reduce time-to-correct on the next item using that term, compared with the identical frame without the popup?\\\" All four slots now hold, and the developer can build both screens.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nOne rejected addition: a teammate proposed also measuring overall lesson enjoyment. I excluded it because enjoyment cannot tell me whether *the arrow* reduced search time; a more enjoyable lesson with a slower start would falsely credit the cue. Keeping one primary measure tied to the hypothesized mechanism is what makes the answer actionable.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nA storyboard note is ready to become a study question only when you can fill all four slots: manipulation, observable, comparison, population. Apply three checks before you treat any note as testable:\n\n1. **Buildability check:** Can you describe the two versions a developer would create? If not, the manipulation is underspecified.\n2. **Falsifiability check:** Can you state, in advance, the result that would make you revise or remove the feature? If no result would change your mind, you have a preference, not a hypothesis.\n3. **Mechanism check:** Does the chosen measure track the reason the feature should work? Enjoyment does not test search; recall does not test motivation.\n\nIf any check fails, the note is not yet evidence-ready, however polished the frame looks.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe frequent error is to treat a more detailed design note as a study question. Adding adjectives (\"a clean, prominent, well-placed arrow\") increases specificity about the *artifact* without adding a measure or a comparison, so the claim remains untestable. A second error is question inflation: asking whether the redesign \"improves learning\" when a single-frame cue change can only plausibly move a narrow, proximal behavior. Scope the question to what the manipulation can actually cause.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the conversion to a fresh case. A storyboard note says: \"Move the narration to start before the diagram appears so learners are prepared.\" First name the manipulation: narration onset relative to diagram onset (before versus simultaneous). Next pick an observable tied to a mechanism: because the temporal contiguity principle predicts that separating related words and pictures in time raises integration cost, the right measure is whether learners correctly match diagram parts to spoken labels, not whether they \"feel prepared.\" Add a comparison (narration-first frame versus simultaneous frame) and a population (learners new to the diagram). The note \"start narration earlier\" becomes: \"For learners new to the diagram, does narration-before-diagram lower the proportion of mislabeled parts, compared with simultaneous presentation?\" That is now buildable, falsifiable, and mechanism-aligned.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nKeep a two-column note: in the left column, the design preference as written; in the right column, the four slots you would need to fill to test it. A strong reader can look at any storyboard annotation and immediately spot which slot is missing (usually the comparison or the observable) and propose the minimal rewrite that supplies it. By the end of this section, you should be able to read a stack of design notes and triage them: which are already testable, which need one slot, and which are pure taste that no tryout could ever adjudicate.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when each design note on the storyboard is paired with a study question whose four slots are filled and whose prespecified decision rule is stated. Visual polish is the last layer; the load-bearing layer is the manipulation-measure-comparison chain. If a note cannot be converted, that is itself a finding: mark it as a preference to resolve by judgment rather than by data, so you do not waste a tryout on a question no result could settle.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same skill to a context with no tryout budget at all. When you cannot run even a five-person test, the conversion still pays off: a precise study question lets you reason from prior evidence. Because Mayer's signaling experiments already show cueing reduces search for novices, a well-scoped question (\"would an arrow reduce search for *these* novices on *this* frame?\") can be answered provisionally from the literature, with the prediction logged so a later tryout can confirm or overturn it. The preference-to-question discipline is what makes borrowed evidence usable instead of ornamental.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design\", \"ail606_supplement_turning_designs_into_study_questions\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/07-01-turning-designs-into-study-questions-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"07.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 07.01. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_turning_designs_into_study_questions\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes records cognitive-load diagnosis, support request, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_turning_designs_into_study_questions","stem":"A storyboard frame shows a branching simulation. Which research question turns that design feature into something a small study could actually answer?","options":["Is the branching simulation engaging and visually appealing?","Does adding the branch point after the narration reduce the number of learners who skip the reader-action prompt, compared with the linear version?","Will learners enjoy the simulation more than a textbook?","Is the storyboard finished and ready to hand off?"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A study question names a design feature (the branch placement), an observable outcome (skipped prompts), and a comparison (linear version). The other options ask for opinions or completion, not evidence.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_polish_as_evidence","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_turning_designs_into_study_questions","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-02"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_turning_designs_into_study_questions","stem":"Which storyboard annotation is specific enough to become a testable study question rather than a vague design hope?","options":["Make the narration nicer.","Signaling cue on frame 3 should cut the time learners spend hunting for the next action; check whether action-start time drops.","This frame feels off.","Add more color to the simulation."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strong annotation names the design element (signaling cue, frame 3), a mechanism (less hunting), and a measure (action-start time). The rest are reactions, not questions.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_outcome_without_construct","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_turning_designs_into_study_questions","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-01","ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-02"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI tool to help you turn a storyboard into a study question. What is the strongest bounded use of that help?","options":["Have the AI write the entire research question and submit it unchanged.","Ask the AI to draft three candidate questions, then keep only the one that names a design element and a measurable learner action you can observe in this storyboard.","Avoid the AI because all study questions must be original.","Use the AI only to make the question sound more academic."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Bounded use means the AI proposes options and you judge them against the storyboard's actual design features and observable outcomes. You stay responsible for the design-to-question link.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_unfalsifiable_question","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_turning_designs_into_study_questions","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-02"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_turning_designs_into_study_questions","stem":"A peer writes the question, \"Does my storyboard improve learning?\" Why is this not yet usable, and what fixes it?","options":["It is fine; learning improvement is the goal of every design.","It names no specific design feature and no observable measure, so no result could confirm or disconfirm it; bind it to one frame change and one learner action you can record.","It is too short; make it a full paragraph.","It needs a more polished storyboard before any question can be asked."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An unfalsifiable question cannot guide revision. Tying it to a single design change and a single observable action makes the result interpretable.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_unfalsifiable_question","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_turning_designs_into_study_questions","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-03","ail606-supplement-07-01-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_polish_as_evidence","pattern":"engagement_as_study_question","description":"Phrasing the study question around appeal or engagement instead of a design feature and an observable learner outcome","trigger":"learner asks whether the storyboard is engaging, appealing, or finished rather than what specific frame change moves a measurable behavior","feedback":"Name the design feature you changed and the learner action you can observe. Engagement is not the variable a small study can interpret.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_outcome_without_construct","pattern":"outcome_without_design_link","description":"Stating a desired outcome without tying it to the specific storyboard element believed to cause it","trigger":"learner names a goal (better learning, less confusion) but no signaling cue, frame, or narration choice that the study would manipulate","feedback":"Connect the outcome to one concrete design element (a signaling cue, a frame order, a reader-action prompt) so the question points at something you can change.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_1_turning_designs_into_study_questions_unfalsifiable_question","pattern":"unfalsifiable_design_claim","description":"Writing a question so broad that no observation could confirm or disconfirm it","trigger":"question uses vague terms like improve learning with no comparison, measure, or bounded design change","feedback":"Bind the question to one design change and one observable action so a result could come out either way.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"2b5436953e563258b96367217c3e9e8eb2476b3df5927a63ba1ca5f3c284fd83","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}